In the UK, {{IPAchar|/aɪ/}} is used more in Southern England, and {{IPAchar|/iː/}} is more usual in Northern England. In North America, {{IPAchar|/iː/}} is most common, but {{IPAchar|/aɪ/}} is predominant in some regions. Note that even if one pronunciation is more common in a region, the pronunciation used varies by individual speaker and sometimes by situation. {{IPAchar|/eɪ/}} was once heard in Northern England, but has now largely fallen into disuse.<ref>"Pronunciation: Either". Reader's Digest. (1964). ''The Complete Atlas of the British Isles'', p. 123.</ref>

===Determiner===
{{en-head|det}}

# Any one (of two).
#: {{cot|en|each|;|both}}
#: {{ux|en|You can have it in '''either''' colour.}}
# Each of two; both. {{defdate|from 9th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|There is a locomotive at '''either''' end of the train, one pulling and the other pushing.}}
#: {{ux|en|There are roses on '''either''' side of the garden.}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=3|passage=His flowing hair <br> In curls on '''either''' cheek played.|year=1873}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1936|author=w:Djuna Barnes|title=w:Nightwood|page=31|publisher=Faber & Faber|year_published=2007
|passage=Her hands, long and beautiful, lay on '''either''' side of her face.}}
# {{lb|en|often|proscribed}} Any one (of more than two).
#* {{cite-book|author=Jonathan Swift|title=Gulliver's Travels|edition=1st|year=1726|passage=I hope you will be ready to own publicly, whenever you shall be called to it, that by your great and frequent urgency you prevailed on me to publish a very loose and uncorrect account of my travels, with directions to hire some young gentleman of '''either''' university to put them in order, and correct the style, as my cousin Dampier did, by my advice, in his book called “A Voyage round the world.”}}
#* {{RQ:Richardson Clarissa|letter=51|text=They ''entreat'', they ''pray'', they ''beg'', they ''supplicate'' (will '''either''' of these do, Miss Clary?) that you will make no scruple to go to your uncle Antony's {{…}}.}}

====Usage notes====
* When there are more than two alternatives, in the sense of “one of several” or “one of many”, {{m|en|any}} is now often used instead. Use of ''either'' with more than two (mutually exclusive) options remains common {{glossary|informal|informally}}<!--a {{glossary|descriptive}} fact-->,<ref name="Pullum-2024">{{cite-book |en |year=2024 |last=Pullum |first=Geoffrey K. |authorlink=Geoffrey K. Pullum |title=The Truth About English Grammar |publisher=Polity Press |page=84-85 |isbn=978-1509560547 |passage=And regardless of how many coordinates there are, when the coordinator is ''or'', you can put the determinative ''either'' in front of it to emphasize the fact that the coordinates present alternatives: ''either toast, hash browns, or pancakes''.}}</ref> but a usage {{glossary|prescriptive|prescription}} against it is so widely known that it is usually avoided {{glossary|formal|formally}}. This fact about ''either'' {{...}} {{m|en|or}} is likewise true of {{m|en|neither}} {{...}} {{m|en|nor}}.

