===Adverb===
{{en-adv|-}}

# {{lb|en|conjunctive|after a negative}} As well.
#: {{ux|en|I don't like him, and I don't like her '''either'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|I know a cheap Spanish restaurant. It's not far from here, '''either'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Heyer Unknown Ajax|chapter=1|passage=But Richmond{{...}}appeared to lose himself in his own reflections. Some pickled crab, which he had not touched, had been removed with a damson pie; and his sister saw{{...}}that he had eaten no more than a spoonful of that '''either'''.}}

====Usage notes====
After a positive statement, {{m|en|too}} is commonly used: “I like him, and I like her too.”

{{m|en|either|Either}} is sometimes used, especially in North American English, where {{m|en|neither}} would be more traditionally accurate: “I’m not hungry.” “Me either.”

